## Scratch paper made of tokens

Several of the quirks in <ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />, like miscounted letters, come from answering straight away: the
first token of the answer has to be right with no working before it. Ask someone for 17 × 24 and
they'll reach for paper. A language model's paper is its own output:
every token it writes goes back in as context for the next one (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />), so writing out the steps
gives later tokens something to build on. Simply asking large models to show their working step by
step, called *chain of thought*, made them better at math word problems.<Cite id="wei2022" />

A <GlossaryPopover term="reasoning-model">reasoning model</GlossaryPopover> does this every time. Before
its answer it writes a stretch of scratch work, which chat apps usually hide or fold away. Here is
Qwen3-0.6B, a small open model whose thinking can be switched off,<Cite id="qwen3" /> on six
puzzles, recorded with thinking off and on:

<ThinkingToggle />

## What the recordings show

**Thinking can fix a mistake.** With thinking off, the model says *strawberry* has 2 r's. With
thinking on, it spells the word out letter by letter, S-T-R-A-W-B-E-R-R-Y, counts 3, and checks
again. Spelling the word out turns tokens it can't see inside (<ChapterRef to="tokens" />) into letters it can count.

**It costs many more tokens.** With thinking on, the model wrote about 24 times as many tokens on
average, mostly scratch work that you never see in the reply.

**It isn't magic, especially in a small model.** Both ways, it got 4 of the 6 right. On two puzzles
the thinking went around in circles, full of "Wait, …", until it hit the limit of 6,000 tokens
without ever answering.

## Trained to think

Models aren't only *asked* to think; they are trained to. One recipe: give the model problems whose
answers can be checked automatically, like math with a single number as the answer, and reward
whatever it wrote when the final answer is right. DeepSeek trained a large model this way. Over
training, its answers grew longer on their own, it began to re-check its steps, and its score on a
hard math competition rose from 15.6% to 71.0%.<Cite id="deepseek2025" /> The model is still
predicting one token at a time. What changed is that it learned which kinds of scratch work tend to
lead to right answers.

Small reasoning models usually learn from a big one instead, by distillation (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />).
DeepSeek tuned small Qwen and Llama models on 800,000 examples made with its big model, and
released them with names like `DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B`. For a 32-billion-weight Qwen model,
this worked much better than training it with rewards directly.<Cite id="deepseek2025" /> Qwen3-0.6B,
the model above, was also tuned to copy the probabilities of larger Qwen3 models.<Cite id="qwen3" />

The recommended settings for Qwen3's thinking mode leave room for up to 32,768 tokens of output, and
warn against always picking the most likely token, which can make it repeat itself
endlessly.<Cite id="qwen3-model-card" /> Thinking is bought with tokens, and tokens take time and
money.

<RecapCard>

- A reasoning model writes scratch work before its answer; every token it writes becomes context for
  the next.
- Writing the steps out can fix mistakes, like counting letters by spelling the word first.
- It costs many more tokens, and it can still go in circles or get the answer wrong.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** knowing how the model works, how do you write prompts that work with it?
